Abstract

We formalize the Agentic Ecological Restoration Architecture (AERA) as a distributionally robust, risk-sensitive, factored partially observable Markov decision process (DR²-fPOMDP) with a constrained multi-agent layer, a cryptographically enforced sovereign data layer, a bistable continuous-time ecosystem dynamics layer, and a spatially coupled leakage model. AERA operationalizes a Minimum-Sufficient Intervention (MSI) criterion: intervention intensity is the minimum multi-dimensional vector necessary to place a degraded parcel on a credibly self-sustaining recovery trajectory, subject to (A1) a recursive CVaR safety envelope over a Wasserstein ambiguity ball, (A2) hard hydrological and state-reliability invariants, (A3) processual Free, Prior and Informed Consent (FPIC), and (A4) Indigenous data sovereignty.

We contribute: (i) a measure-theoretic foundation (§2); (ii) a factored state factorization with explicit DBN topology (§3); (iii) a governance-invariant FPIC algebra without belief-mass loopholes (§4); (iv) a distributionally robust Bellman operator with a γ-contraction proof under sa-rectangularity and weak-* compactness (§5); (v) a recursive CVaR risk operator that composes with the robust Bellman operator by construction, plus a measure-based smoothing bound (§6); (vi) a lexicographic multi-agent elimination protocol with finite-time convergence and scale-invariant disagreement (§7); (vii) a Sovereign Data Supply Chain with an explicit oracle-trust boundary (§8); (viii) a life-cycle economic layer with a corrected UAV/manual inversion threshold and equal-marginal MEROI allocation (§9); (ix) a bistable Allee-type dynamics layer with Lyapunov and ISS theorems for the autonomous regime (§10); (x) a spatial drift–diffusion leakage model with a net-positive-gain condition (§11); and (xi) a pre-registered six-phase experimental architecture (§12).

