2. Axiomatic and Topological Foundations

Every subsequent theorem depends on the underlying space being a Polish space (separable, complete metric) with a Borel σ-algebra, and on transition kernels being measurable with respect to that σ-algebra.

2.1 State, action, and observation spaces

Let $(\mathcal{X}, d_{\mathcal{X}})$ be a Polish space — the latent ecological state space. Let $\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{X})$ denote its Borel σ-algebra. Let $(\mathcal{A}, d_{\mathcal{A}})$ be a compact metric space of actions. Let $(\mathcal{Y}, d_{\mathcal{Y}})$ be a Polish observation space.

The belief space is
\mathcal{B} \;=\; \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X}),


the space of Borel probability measures on $\mathcal{X}$, equipped with the weak- topology* induced by the bounded continuous functions $C_b(\mathcal{X})$.

Standing Assumption (SP). $\mathcal{X}$ is compact; $\mathcal{A}$ is compact; $\mathcal{Y}$ is Polish. Under (SP), $\mathcal{B}$ is compact in the weak-* topology by Prokhorov's theorem.

2.2 Kernels, strategies, and probability space

A Markov transition kernel is a map
T : \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X}), \qquad (x,a) \mapsto T(\cdot \mid x, a),


such that for every $B \in \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{X})$, the map $(x,a) \mapsto T(B \mid x,a)$ is measurable, and for every $(x,a)$, $T(\cdot \mid x,a) \in \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X})$.

An observation kernel $O : \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{Y})$ is defined analogously.

A policy is a universally measurable map $\pi : \mathcal{B} \to \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{A})$ (for randomized policies) or $\pi : \mathcal{B} \to \mathcal{A}$ (deterministic).

The canonical filtered probability space is
(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \{\mathcal{F}_t\}_{t \geq 0}, \mathbb{P}^{\pi, T}),


satisfying the usual conditions (right-continuous, complete). Trajectories $\tau = (X_0, a_0, X_1, a_1, \ldots)$ are $\mathcal{F}$-measurable.

2.3 Weak-* compactness and measurable selection

Lemma 2.1 (Prokhorov). If $(\mathcal{X}, d_{\mathcal{X}})$ is Polish and $\bar{T}(\cdot \mid x, a)$ is tight for all $(x,a) \in \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{A}$, then for any $\rho \geq 0$ the Wasserstein ball
\mathcal{P}_\rho(\bar{T}(\cdot \mid x,a)) = \{P \in \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X}) : \mathcal{W}_1(P, \bar{T}(\cdot \mid x,a)) \leq \rho\}


is weak-* compact.

Lemma 2.2 (Measurable selection; Jankov–von Neumann). Let $F : \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{A} \rightrightarrows \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X})$ be a set-valued map with nonempty compact values, upper semicontinuous in the Hausdorff metric on $\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X})$. Then $F$ admits a universally measurable selector.

Lemma 2.3 (Measurability of the sup-map). Under Lemma 2.1, if $\bar{T}(\cdot \mid x,a)$ and $\rho(x,a)$ are continuous in $(x,a)$, and $V : \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}$ is bounded and upper semicontinuous, then the map
(x,a) \mapsto \sup_{P \in \mathcal{P}_\rho(\bar{T}(\cdot \mid x,a))} \mathbb{E}_P[V]


is upper semicontinuous in $(x,a)$.

Proof. Upper semicontinuity of $V$ implies $P \mapsto \mathbb{E}_P[V]$ is upper semicontinuous on $\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X})$ under weak-* convergence. The map $(x,a) \mapsto \mathcal{P}_\rho(\bar{T}(\cdot \mid x,a))$ is upper semicontinuous in the Hausdorff metric (Berge's Maximum Theorem). Compose. ∎

2.4 Function spaces

$\mathcal{V}_b(\mathcal{X}) := \{V : \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R} \mid \|V\|_\infty = \sup_{x \in \mathcal{X}} |V(x)| < \infty\}$ is a Banach space under $\|\cdot\|_\infty$. Under (SP), $\mathcal{V}_b(\mathcal{X})$ is closed under all operators defined below.

