3. State-Space Factorization and Belief Dynamics

3.1 Factored latent state

Each parcel $i$ is represented by a latent state
X_t^{(i)} = (S_t, W_t, B_t, F_t, C_t, H_t, G_t, R_t) \in \mathcal{X} = \textstyle\prod_{k \in \mathcal{K}} \mathcal{X}^{(k)},


with $\mathcal{K} = \{S, W, B, F, C, H, G, R\}$ and each $\mathcal{X}^{(k)}$ Polish and compact.

3.2 Dynamic Bayesian Network factorization

Let $\mathcal{G} = (\mathcal{K}, \mathcal{E})$ be a DAG with parent map $\mathrm{Pa} : \mathcal{K} \to 2^\mathcal{K}$, representing a two-time-slice DBN in which all parents are at time $t$ and all children at time $t+1$.

Lemma 3.1 (DBN factorization). If the latent process is Markov with respect to $\mathcal{G}$, then
T(X_{t+1} \mid X_t, a_t) = \prod_{k \in \mathcal{K}} P(X_{t+1}^{(k)} \mid \mathrm{Pa}(X_{t+1}^{(k)}), a_t).

Proof. Standard DBN factorization via the local Markov property on $\mathcal{G}$. ∎

The tropical-forest coupling topology used in this draft is
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{Pa}(S) &= \{S\}, & \mathrm{Pa}(W) &= \{W, S, C\}, \\
\mathrm{Pa}(B) &= \{B, S, W, F\}, & \mathrm{Pa}(F) &= \{F, B, H\}, \\
\mathrm{Pa}(C) &= \{C, B, W\}, & \mathrm{Pa}(H) &= \{H, B, F\}, \\
\mathrm{Pa}(G) &= \{G\}, & \mathrm{Pa}(R) &= \{R, W, B, C\}.
\end{aligned}

Remark 3.2. This DAG is a working hypothesis. Structural edges must be validated empirically (§14.2).

3.3 Observation model

Measurements $Y_t = (Y_t^{\text{sat}}, Y_t^{\text{drone}}, Y_t^{\text{field}})$ are conditionally independent across modalities:
O(Y_t \mid X_t) = \prod_{m} O^{(m)}(Y_t^{(m)} \mid X_t^{(m)}),


with $Y_t^{(m)} = h^{(m)}(X_t) + \varepsilon_t^{(m)}$, $\varepsilon_t^{(m)} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma_t^{(m)})$.

Canopy attenuation. For drones,
h^{(\text{drone})}(X_t) = \mu_{\text{canopy}} \cdot h^{(\text{clear})}(X_t), \qquad \mu_{\text{canopy}} \in (0,1].

3.4 Belief update

The Bayes belief update is
b_{t+1}(X_{t+1}) = \eta \, O(Y_{t+1} \mid X_{t+1}) \int_{\mathcal{X}} T(X_{t+1} \mid X_t, a_t) \, b_t(X_t) \, dX_t,


with normalizing constant $\eta = 1 / \mathbb{P}(Y_{t+1} \mid b_t, a_t)$.

Lemma 3.3 (Belief measurability). Under (SP) and continuity of $O$ in $Y$, the map $(b_t, a_t, Y_{t+1}) \mapsto b_{t+1}$ is jointly measurable.

Proof. Standard; the conditional expectation of a bounded measurable function against a transition kernel is measurable in $(b, a, Y)$ by the monotone class theorem. ∎

