4. Governance-Constrained Constraint Algebra

4.1 Hydrological constraint

For a joint action $a$ and belief $b_t$,
C_{\text{Hydro}}(a, b_t) = \mathbb{E}_{X \sim b_t}[W_{\text{demand}}(a) - (W_t + \Delta W_{\text{recycle}})].


The hydrological constraint is satisfied iff $C_{\text{Hydro}}(a, b_t) \leq 0$.

4.2 Territorial membership is deterministic

Definition 4.1 (Territorial membership). Let $\mathcal{I} = \{1, \ldots, N\}$ be the parcel index set. The territorial membership map
\mathsf{T} : \mathcal{I} \to \{0, 1\}


is a deterministic input from cadastral and GIS records, fixed at the start of each planning epoch. $\mathsf{T}(i) = 1$ iff parcel $i$ lies within the footprint of a recognized Indigenous territory.

Remark 4.2. Territorial membership is not a belief-mass quantity. Prior drafts (v4, v5) encoded FPIC over $\mathrm{Supp}_\varepsilon(b_t)$, which silently excluded parcels with low belief mass from a hard governance constraint. v6 removes this loophole: FPIC binds on the deterministic set $\mathsf{T}^{-1}(1)$ regardless of belief.

4.3 FPIC constraint operator

Definition 4.3 (FPIC authorization). Consent is a time-varying authorization matrix
\Theta_t^{\text{FPIC}} = \{(i, a, \text{Scope}, \tau_{\text{expire}}) : \mathsf{T}(i) = 1, \; a \in \mathcal{A}, \; \text{Scope} \subseteq \mathcal{K}_{\text{actions}}, \; \tau_{\text{expire}} > t\}.

For joint action $a_t \in \mathcal{A}$ and territorial parcel set $\mathcal{I}_{\text{ind}} := \mathsf{T}^{-1}(1)$:
C_{\text{FPIC}}(a_t) = \prod_{i \in \mathcal{I}_{\text{ind}}} \mathbb{1}[a_t \in \mathrm{ApprovedActions}(\Theta_t^{\text{FPIC}}, i)].

Axiom 4.4 (A-FPIC-PASSIVE). We adopt the modeling axiom that the passive default action $a_{\text{passive}} = (P, \ldots, P)$ is authorized on every parcel whenever no active consent entry exists for that parcel. This is a modeling choice, not a claim about customary FPIC. Deployments must calibrate A-FPIC-PASSIVE against each territory's customary protocol.

4.4 Local revocation

Definition 4.5 (Revocation operator). For territory index $i^*$ and action $a^*$ at time $t_r$,
\mathcal{R}_{\text{consent}}(\Theta_t^{\text{FPIC}}, i^*, a^*) \implies \Theta_{t^+}^{\text{FPIC}} = \Theta_t^{\text{FPIC}} \setminus \{(i^*, a^*, \cdot, \cdot)\}, \qquad \forall t^+ \geq t_r.

Theorem 4.6 (Local revocation invariance). If consent is revoked at $t_r$ for parcel $i^*$ and action $a^*$, then for all $t^+ \geq t_r$, $C_{\text{FPIC}}(a^*, b_{t^+}) = 0$.

Proof. Since $\mathsf{T}(i^*) = 1$ (deterministic), $i^* \in \mathcal{I}_{\text{ind}}$. By Definition 4.5, the tuple $(i^*, a^*, \cdot, \cdot)$ is removed. The factor $\mathbb{1}[a^* \in \mathrm{ApprovedActions}(\Theta_{t^+}^{\text{FPIC}}, i^*)] = 0$, so the product is zero. ∎

Corollary 4.7 (Global fallback). If the feasible set $\mathcal{A}_1 = \{a : C_{\text{FPIC}}(a) = 1, C_{\text{Hydro}}(a, b_t) \leq 0\}$ is empty, the lexicographic operator $\Pi_{\mathcal{L}_1}$ returns $a_{\text{passive}}$, which by Axiom 4.4 satisfies $C_{\text{FPIC}}(a_{\text{passive}}) = 1$ and $C_{\text{Hydro}}(a_{\text{passive}}, b_t) \leq 0$.

Proof. Immediate from Definition of $\Pi_{\mathcal{L}_1}$ and Axiom 4.4. ∎

Remark 4.8. The G1 guarantee in §13 is restated to match what is actually proven: conditional on A-FPIC-PASSIVE, no autonomous action violates FPIC on any parcel with $\mathsf{T}(i) = 1$. Deployments without this axiom must replace Corollary 4.7 with a territory-specific fallback.

