5. Distributionally Robust Bellman Operator

5.1 Ambiguity set

Let $\bar{T}(\cdot \mid x, a)$ be a nominal transition kernel. Define the sa-rectangular Wasserstein ambiguity set
\mathcal{P}_\rho(\bar{T}) = \bigotimes_{(x,a)} \left\{ P(\cdot \mid x,a) \in \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X}) \;:\; \mathcal{W}_1(P(\cdot \mid x,a), \bar{T}(\cdot \mid x,a)) \leq \rho(x,a) \right\}.

Assumption 5.1 (A-RECT). The ambiguity set is sa-rectangular: conditional transition probabilities at $(x,a)$ do not depend on transitions at other $(x', a')$.

5.2 Robust Bellman operator

Definition 5.2 (Robust Bellman operator). For $V \in \mathcal{V}_b(\mathcal{X})$,
(\mathbf{T}^* V)(b) = \min_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \left\{ c(b, a) + \gamma \sup_{P \in \mathcal{P}_\rho(\bar{T})} \mathbb{E}_P[V(b')] \right\},


where $b' = b'(b, a, Y, P)$ is the Bayesian belief update under $P$.

5.3 Well-posedness, contraction, and measurability

Lemma 5.3 (Well-posedness — Lemma A). Under (SP) and Lemma 2.1, the inner supremum in Definition 5.2 is attained for every $(b, a)$.

Proof. $\mathcal{P}_\rho(\bar{T})$ is weak-* compact (Lemma 2.1). $P \mapsto \mathbb{E}_P[V]$ is weak-* continuous for bounded continuous $V$. A continuous function on a compact set attains its supremum. ∎

Lemma 5.4 (Measurable selection — Lemma B). Under Lemma 2.1 and Lemma 2.3, the map $(b, a) \mapsto \sup_{P} \mathbb{E}_P[V(b')]$ is upper semicontinuous in $(b, a)$, and the argmin over $a \in \mathcal{A}$ is universally measurable.

Proof. Upper semicontinuity follows from Lemma 2.3. Universal measurability of the argmin follows from the Jankov–von Neumann selection theorem (Lemma 2.2) applied to the upper-semicontinuous set-valued map $a \mapsto \arg\min$. ∎

Theorem 5.5 (Strict γ-contraction). Under (SP), (A-RECT), Lemma 5.3, and Lemma 5.4, $\mathbf{T}^*$ is a strict γ-contraction in $\|\cdot\|_\infty$:
\|\mathbf{T}^* V_1 - \mathbf{T}^* V_2\|_\infty \leq \gamma \|V_1 - V_2\|_\infty.

Proof. Fix $b$. Let $a_1^*, a_2^*$ be minimizers for $V_1, V_2$. Then
(\mathbf{T}^* V_1)(b) - (\mathbf{T}^* V_2)(b) \leq \gamma \left( \sup_P \mathbb{E}_P[V_1(b')] - \sup_P \mathbb{E}_P[V_2(b')] \right) \leq \gamma \sup_P \mathbb{E}_P[|V_1(b') - V_2(b')|] \leq \gamma \|V_1 - V_2\|_\infty.


Symmetrically for the reverse. Take $\sup_b$. Banach's fixed-point theorem gives existence and uniqueness of $V^* \in \mathcal{V}_b(\mathcal{X})$. ∎

Corollary 5.6 (Value iteration convergence). For any $V_0 \in \mathcal{V}_b(\mathcal{X})$,
\|V_n - V^*\|_\infty \leq \gamma^n \|V_0 - V^*\|_\infty.

