6. Risk-Sensitive Optimization: Recursive CVaR and MSI

6.1 The composition problem

The v5 draft defined a static trajectory CVaR $\mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha^{\mathcal{P}}(L_\varepsilon)$ where $L_\varepsilon$ was a sum over the entire trajectory and $\mathcal{P}$ was a per-step transition-kernel ambiguity set. This is a domain mismatch: $P \in \mathcal{P}$ is not a distribution over trajectories, so $\mathbb{E}_P[(L_\varepsilon - \zeta)^+]$ is not well-defined.

There are two standard resolutions:

· (R1) Recursive (Markov) risk. Define a stage-wise CVaR operator that is dynamically time-consistent by construction.
· (R2) Static trajectory CVaR with lifted ambiguity. Define a separate ambiguity set over trajectory distributions and apply CVaR to it.

v6 adopts (R1) as the primary optimization object (it composes with the robust Bellman operator by construction) and retains (R2) as a post-hoc evaluation metric.

6.2 Recursive CVaR risk operator

Definition 6.1 (Recursive CVaR). For a confidence level $\alpha \in (0,1)$, define the stage-wise operator
(\mathbf{R}_\alpha V)(b) = \min_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \left\{ c(b, a) + \gamma \, \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha^{P \in \mathcal{P}_\rho(\bar{T})}\!\left[ V(b') \right] \right\},


where the CVaR is taken over the belief-induced distribution of $b'$ under $P \in \mathcal{P}_\rho(\bar{T})$:
\mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha^{P}\!\left[ V(b') \right] = \inf_{\zeta \in \mathbb{R}} \left\{ \zeta + \frac{1}{1-\alpha} \sup_{P \in \mathcal{P}_\rho(\bar{T})} \mathbb{E}_P\!\left[(V(b') - \zeta)^+\right] \right\}.

Theorem 6.2 (Composition and contraction). Under (SP), (A-RECT), Lemmas 5.3 and 5.4, and the additional assumption that $V \mapsto \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha[V]$ is $1$-Lipschitz under $\|\cdot\|_\infty$ (which holds for CVaR), the operator $\mathbf{R}_\alpha$ is a $\gamma$-contraction in $\|\cdot\|_\infty$.

Proof. The CVaR operator is $1$-Lipschitz in its argument under $\|\cdot\|_\infty$ (Rockafellar & Uryasev, 2000, Prop. 4). Apply the same argument as Theorem 5.5. ∎

Remark 6.3 (No A-TIME-CONSIST needed). The recursive CVaR operator is dynamically time-consistent by construction (Ruszczyński, 2010, Thm. 3.1). The v5 assumption A-TIME-CONSIST is therefore not required for the primary optimization. It is required only when interpreting static trajectory CVaR as a guarantee on the recursively-optimal policy — see §6.5.

6.3 MSI optimization program (v6)

\pi^* = \arg\min_{\pi \in \Pi} \; \mathbb{E}_{\pi, \bar{T}}\!\left[ \sum_{t=0}^\infty \gamma^t \|I(a_t)\|_W \right]

subject to

\begin{aligned}
\text{(A1)} \quad & \text{Recursive CVaR envelope: } (\mathbf{R}_\alpha V_{\text{fail}})(b_t) \leq \varepsilon_{\text{risk}} \quad \forall t, \\
\text{(A2)} \quad & \inf_{P \in \mathcal{P}_\rho(\bar{T})} \Pr_P(W_t \geq W_{\min} \wedge B_t \geq B_{\min} \wedge R_t \geq R_{\min}) \geq 1 - \delta, \\
\text{(A3)} \quad & C_{\text{FPIC}}(a_t) = 1, \\
\text{(A4)} \quad & C_{\text{Hydro}}(a_t, b_t) \leq 0, \\
\text{(A5)} \quad & P_t \in \mathcal{P}_\rho(\bar{T}), \\
\text{(A6)} \quad & \text{(A-RECT) holds.}
\end{aligned}

Here $V_{\text{fail}}$ is the failure value function, defined as the discounted sum of smoothed failure costs under $\pi$.

Remark 6.4 (Feasibility). Feasibility is guaranteed by $a_{\text{passive}}$ under Axiom 4.4.

6.4 Failure smoothing: measure-based bound

Definition 6.5 (Smoothed failure cost). Fix $\varepsilon_L > 0$ and a Lipschitz non-increasing surrogate $\varphi : \mathbb{R} \to [0,1]$ with $\|\varphi'\|_\infty \leq 1$. Define the signed distance
d_{\text{unsafe}}(X) = \mathrm{dist}(X, \mathcal{X}_{\text{unsafe}}) - \mathrm{dist}(X, \mathcal{X} \setminus \mathcal{X}_{\text{unsafe}}),


and the smoothed failure cost
c_{\text{fail}}^\varepsilon(X, a) = \varphi(d_{\text{unsafe}}(X) / \varepsilon_L).

Assumption 6.6 (A-BOUNDARY). The failure boundary $\partial \mathcal{X}_{\text{unsafe}}$ has zero mass under every $P \in \mathcal{P}_\rho(\bar{T})$. Equivalently, for any $\varepsilon > 0$, $P(\{X : |d_{\text{unsafe}}(X)| < \varepsilon\}) \to 0$ as $\varepsilon \to 0$, and there exists $C_{\text{bd}} > 0$ such that $P(\{X : |d_{\text{unsafe}}(X)| \leq \varepsilon_L\}) \leq C_{\text{bd}} \varepsilon_L$ for all $P \in \mathcal{P}_\rho(\bar{T})$.

Lemma 6.7 (Smoothed-unsmoothed difference). Under Assumption 6.6,
\mathbb{E}_P[|c_{\text{fail}}^\varepsilon(X, a) - c_{\text{fail}}(X, a)|] \leq C_{\text{bd}} \varepsilon_L, \qquad \forall P \in \mathcal{P}_\rho(\bar{T}).

Proof. $|c_{\text{fail}}^\varepsilon - c_{\text{fail}}| \leq \mathbb{1}\{|d_{\text{unsafe}}(X)| \leq \varepsilon_L\}$ for $\varphi$ Lipschitz with $\varphi(0) \in (0,1)$. Take expectations and apply Assumption 6.6. ∎

Corollary 6.8 (Trajectory CVaR slack). Let $L_\varepsilon(\tau) = \sum_{t=0}^T \gamma^t c_{\text{fail}}^\varepsilon(X_t, a_t)$ and $L(\tau)$ its unsmoothed counterpart. Then
|\mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha^P(L) - \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha^P(L_\varepsilon)| \leq C_{\text{bd}} \varepsilon_L \cdot \frac{1 - \gamma^{T+1}}{1 - \gamma}.

Proof. CVaR is $1$-Lipschitz in the loss under $\|\cdot\|_\infty$. Combine with Lemma 6.7. ∎

Remark 6.9. Unlike the v5 bound, this slack shrinks with $\varepsilon_L$ and vanishes as $\varepsilon_L \to 0$ under Assumption 6.6. This is the correct trade-off structure: smaller smoothing scale, tighter guarantee.

6.5 Static trajectory CVaR as evaluation metric

For deployment verification, a static trajectory CVaR can be applied to the recursively-optimal policy. Define the trajectory ambiguity set
\mathfrak{P} = \left\{ \mathbb{P}^{\pi^*, P}_{\text{traj}} : P \in \mathcal{P}_\rho(\bar{T}) \right\} \subset \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{T}),


where $\mathcal{T}$ is the trajectory space. Then define
\mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha^{\mathfrak{P}}(L_\varepsilon) = \inf_{\zeta} \left\{ \zeta + \frac{1}{1-\alpha} \sup_{\mathbb{P} \in \mathfrak{P}} \mathbb{E}_{\mathbb{P}}[(L_\varepsilon - \zeta)^+] \right\}.


This is a well-defined functional. It is an evaluation metric, not the constraint. It may exceed $\varepsilon_{\text{risk}}$ even when the recursive constraint (A1) holds — the two are not equivalent without additional structural assumptions. In deployment, (A1) is the hard constraint; $\mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha^{\mathfrak{P}}$ is reported as a diagnostic.

