9. Economic Architecture

9.1 Continuous-time life-cycle cost

For policy $\pi$ and parcel trajectory $x(t)$,
J_{\text{cost}}(\pi) = \mathbb{E}_{\pi, \mathcal{P}}\!\left[ C_{\text{diag}}(x_0) + \int_0^{T_{\text{hor}}} e^{-rt} \left( C_{\text{interv}}(u(t)) + C_{\text{maint}}(x(t), u(t)) + C_{\text{mon}}(y(t)) + C_{\text{risk}}(x(t), t) \right) dt \right],


with
C_{\text{risk}}(x, t) = \sum_{d} \lambda_d(x,t) \mathbb{E}[D_d(x)], \qquad C_{\text{maint}}(x, u) = C_0 (1 - u_{\text{passive}}) e^{-\beta E(x)}.


Sensitivity at $r = 0.02$ and $r = 0.06$.

9.2 UAV vs manual planting: corrected inversion

Let $A$ = parcel area (ha), $\rho_{\text{density}}$ = target density (plants/ha), $c_{\text{sapling}}$ = unit sapling cost, $c_{\text{labor}}$ = labor cost, $\eta_{\text{terrain}} \geq 1$ = terrain penalty, $q_{\text{rate}}$ = seed rate, $c_{\text{seed}}$ = seed cost, $c_{\text{flight}}$ = per-flight cost, $F_{\text{manual}}, F_{\text{UAV}}$ = fixed costs, $\mu$ = seed survival, $\theta_{\text{target}}$ = target establishment.

C_{\text{manual}}(A, \eta) = A(\rho_{\text{density}} c_{\text{sapling}} + c_{\text{labor}} \eta) + F_{\text{manual}},


C_{\text{UAV}}(A, n) = A n (q_{\text{rate}} c_{\text{seed}} + c_{\text{flight}}) + F_{\text{UAV}},


n^* = \left\lceil \frac{\log(1 - \theta_{\text{target}})}{\log(1 - \mu)} \right\rceil.

Proposition 9.1 (UAV economic inversion threshold). Assume (i) $\mu, \theta_{\text{target}} \in (0,1)$, (ii) the continuous relaxation of $n^*$ is used. Define
D := \rho_{\text{density}} c_{\text{sapling}} + c_{\text{labor}} \eta_{\text{terrain}} + \frac{F_{\text{manual}} - F_{\text{UAV}}}{A}.

· If $D > 0$: $C_{\text{UAV}} < C_{\text{manual}}$ iff $\mu > 1 - (1 - \theta_{\text{target}})^{(q_{\text{rate}} c_{\text{seed}} + c_{\text{flight}})/D}$.
· If $D \leq 0$: $C_{\text{manual}} < C_{\text{UAV}}$ for all $\mu \in (0,1)$. Manual planting dominates UAV.

Proof. $C_{\text{manual}} - C_{\text{UAV}} = A[D - n^*(q_{\text{rate}} c_{\text{seed}} + c_{\text{flight}})]$. If $D > 0$, UAV wins iff $n^* < A D / (A(q_{\text{rate}} c_{\text{seed}} + c_{\text{flight}}))$, i.e., $\log(1-\theta_{\text{target}}) / \log(1-\mu) < D / (q_{\text{rate}} c_{\text{seed}} + c_{\text{flight}})$. Both logs are negative; multiply by $\log(1-\mu)$ (flips inequality) and exponentiate. If $D \leq 0$, the bracket is strictly negative, so $C_{\text{manual}} < C_{\text{UAV}}$. ∎

Remark 9.2. v5 stated the $D \leq 0$ case backwards. The corrected version is above.

9.3 MEROI allocation

Define
\mathrm{MEROI}_i(u_i) = \partial_{u_i} \mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{P}_i}[E(X_{t+\tau}^{(i)} \mid b_t^{(i)}, \pi(u_i))].


The global allocation solves
u^* = \arg\max_{u \geq 0} \sum_i w_i^{\text{prio}} \Delta E_i(u_i) - \gamma_{\text{risk}} \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha^{\mathcal{P}}\!\left(\sum_i L_i(u_i)\right)


subject to $\sum_i u_i \leq B_{\text{global}}$ and $u_i \geq C_{\text{protection}}^{(i)} \mathbb{1}(G_t^{(i)} = 1)$.

Theorem 9.3 (Equal-marginal allocation). Assume (i) each $\Delta E_i$ is concave in $u_i$; (ii) the risk term is convex in $u$; (iii) $w_i^{\text{prio}} > 0$ for all $i$; (iv) CVaR is differentiable at the optimum. Then at optimality
\mathrm{MEROI}_i^*(u_i^*) = \frac{\lambda^* + \gamma_{\text{risk}} \partial_{u_i} \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha^{\mathcal{P}}}{w_i^{\text{prio}}}, \qquad \forall i \text{ with } u_i^* > C_{\text{protection}}^{(i)}.

Proof. KKT on the Lagrangian. Convexity ensures global optimality. ∎

Caveat 9.4 (Convexity). Concavity of $\Delta E_i$ in $u_i$ is a modeling assumption. Non-concave cases require mixed-integer or global methods.

9.4 Participatory weight elicitation

Protocol 9.5. For each territorial council, elicit:

1. Ecological index weights $w$ (soil, water, biodiversity, carbon, fauna) via participatory MCDA with pairwise comparisons.
2. Parcel priority weights $w_i^{\text{prio}}$ via a pre-registered procedure combining territorial ranking, transparent baseline, and an appeals mechanism.
3. Intensity-metric weights $w_1, w_2, w_3$ via the same MCDA instrument, with explicit framing of cost/social-risk/habitat-footprint trade-offs.

Weights are logged on the ledger and revocable under the same FPIC protocol as actions (Definition 4.5).

