10. Ecosystem Self-Sustainability and Technological Exit

10.1 Continuous-time dynamics

Let $E(t) \in \mathcal{X}_E = (0,1]$ denote scalar functional ecological integrity. The dynamics are
\dot{E}(t) = f_0(E(t)) + g(E(t)) u(t) - d_{\text{ext}}(t), \qquad E(0) = E_0 \in \mathcal{X}_E,


where:

· $f_0 : [0,1] \to \mathbb{R}$ is a $C^2$ vector field (intrinsic recovery, Allee dynamics);
· $g : [0,1] \to [\delta_g, G_{\max}]$, $\delta_g > 0$, is $C^1$ (intervention coupling);
· $u(t) \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$ is active intervention intensity;
· $d_{\text{ext}}(t) \in [0, d_{\max}]$ is Lebesgue-measurable exogenous disturbance.

10.2 Bistability: existence assumption (corrected)

Assumption 10.1 (A-BISTABLE). The unforced vector field $f_0$ possesses exactly three equilibria in $[0,1]$: $E = 0$, $E = E_{\text{low}}$, $E = E_{\text{mature}}$, with
0 < E_{\text{low}} < E_{\text{mature}} \leq 1, \quad f_0(0) = f_0(E_{\text{low}}) = f_0(E_{\text{mature}}) = 0,


f_0(E) < 0 \text{ on } (0, E_{\text{low}}) \cup (E_{\text{mature}}, 1], \quad f_0(E) > 0 \text{ on } (E_{\text{low}}, E_{\text{mature}}),


and $f_0'(E_{\text{low}}) = \lambda_{\text{low}} > 0$, $f_0'(E_{\text{mature}}) = -m_0 < 0$ with $m_0 > 0$.

Remark 10.2. v5 conflated $E^*$ (the Allee threshold of the Allee component) with a root of the full $f_0$. Under Assumption 10.1, the existence of $E_{\text{low}}$ and $E_{\text{mature}}$ is a hypothesis — not a derivation. It must be verified per parcel from field data (§14.2).

10.3 Autonomous local asymptotic stability

Theorem 10.3 (LAS and exponential convergence). Let Assumption 10.1 hold, and assume $d_{\text{ext}}(t) \equiv 0$. Then $E_{\text{mature}}$ is locally exponentially stable: for any $E(0) \in \mathcal{B}_\eta(E_{\text{mature}})$ with sufficiently small $\eta > 0$, there exist $c_0 > 0$ such that
|E(t) - E_{\text{mature}}| \leq |E(0) - E_{\text{mature}}| e^{-c_0 t/2}.

Proof. Set $x(t) = E(t) - E_{\text{mature}}$. By Taylor expansion at $E_{\text{mature}}$,
f_0(E) = -m_0 x + R_2(x), \qquad |R_2(x)| \leq \tfrac{1}{2} M_2 x^2,


with $M_2 = \max_{[0,1]} |f_0''|$. Let $V(x) = \tfrac{1}{2} x^2$. Then
\dot{V} = x(-m_0 x + R_2(x)) \leq -m_0 x^2 + \tfrac{1}{2} M_2 |x|^3.


Restrict $|x| \leq \eta := m_0(1-\theta)/M_2$ for $\theta \in (0,1)$. Then
\dot{V} \leq -m_0 x^2 + \tfrac{1}{2} m_0(1-\theta) x^2 = -\tfrac{1}{2} m_0 (1+\theta) x^2 = -c_0 V,


with $c_0 = m_0(1+\theta) > 0$. Grönwall gives $V(x(t)) \leq V(x(0)) e^{-c_0 t}$, hence $|x(t)| \leq |x(0)| e^{-c_0 t/2}$. ∎

10.4 Input-to-state stability

Theorem 10.4 (ISS). Let Assumption 10.1 hold and $d_{\text{ext}}(t) \leq d_{\max}$ for all $t$. Then the ecosystem is input-to-state stable:
|E(t) - E_{\text{mature}}| \leq \beta(|E(0) - E_{\text{mature}}|, t) + \gamma_{\text{ISS}}\!\left( \sup_{s \in [0,t]} d_{\text{ext}}(s) \right),


with $\beta \in \mathcal{KL}$ and
\gamma_{\text{ISS}}(r) = \frac{4 r}{m_0 (1-\theta)}.

Proof. Same Lyapunov function; decompose the negative quadratic term with $\theta_1 \in (0,1)$:
\dot{V} \leq -(1-\theta_1) c_1 |x|^2 - \theta_1 c_1 |x|^2 + |x| d_{\text{ext}}(t),


with $c_1 = \tfrac{1}{2} m_0 (1-\theta)$. Whenever $|x| \geq d_{\text{ext}}(t)/(\theta_1 c_1)$, the last two terms are $\leq 0$. By Sontag's ISS lemma,
|x(t)| \leq |x(0)| e^{-(1-\theta_1) c_1 t} + \frac{1}{\theta_1 c_1} \sup_{s} d_{\text{ext}}(s).


Setting $\theta_1 = 1/2$ gives $\gamma_{\text{ISS}}(r) = 2r/c_1 = 4r/(m_0(1-\theta))$. ∎

10.5 Finite-time technological exit

When $E(0) < E_{\text{low}}$, the parcel cannot self-recover. Define the MSI control law
u(E) = \begin{cases} \dfrac{-f_0(E) + \kappa (E_{\text{target}} - E) + d_{\max}}{g(E)}, & E < E_{\text{target}}, \\ 0, & E \geq E_{\text{target}}, \end{cases}


with $E_{\text{target}} := E_{\text{low}} + \delta$ and $\kappa > 0$.

Theorem 10.5 (Finite-time exit and exponential intervention decay). Under the MSI control law, for any $E(0) \in (0, E_{\text{target}})$:

1. $E(t)$ reaches $E_{\text{target}} - \epsilon$ in finite time
   T_{\text{exit}}(\epsilon) = \frac{1}{\kappa} \ln\!\left( \frac{E_{\text{target}} - E(0)}{\epsilon} \right) < \infty.
2. For all $t \geq T_{\text{exit}}$, $u(t) \equiv 0$, so $\|I(u(t))\|_W = 0$.

Proof. Substitute $u(E)$ into the dynamics:
\dot{E} = \kappa (E_{\text{target}} - E) + (d_{\max} - d_{\text{ext}}(t)) \geq \kappa (E_{\text{target}} - E).


Set $e(t) = E_{\text{target}} - E(t) > 0$. Then $\dot{e} \leq -\kappa e$, so $e(t) \leq e(0) e^{-\kappa t}$. Finite-time exit follows. At $t \geq T_{\text{exit}}$, $E \geq E_{\text{target}}$, so $u \equiv 0$. ∎

Remark 10.6. Theorem 10.5 makes the "technological exit" claim rigorous: active intervention is a temporary catalyst with a bounded duration, after which the ecological system autonomously converges to $E_{\text{mature}}$ by Theorem 10.3.

