a_t^* &= \pi^*(b_t) = \arg\min_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \sup_{P \in \mathcal{P}_\rho(\bar{T})} \mathbb{E}_P[J_{\text{cost}}(a, b_t)] \; \text{s.t. } \Pi_{\mathcal{L}_1}(a_t^*) = a_t^*, && \text{[Robust planning \& governance]} \\
\dot{E}(x,t) &= f_0(E(x,t)) + g(E(x,t)) a_t^* - d_{\text{ext}}(x,t), && \text{[Continuous ecological dynamics]} \\
\frac{d\mathcal{G}_{\text{net}}}{dt} &= \int_{\Omega_{\text{rest}}} \dot{E} \, dA - \int_{\Omega_{\text{risk}}} \lambda_{\text{defor}} E \, dA - \mathcal{L}_{\text{spillover}}, && \text{[Basin-wide macro synthesis]}
\end{aligned}

13.2 Architectural guarantees

G1 (Safety and sovereignty invariance). Under Axiom 4.4 (A-FPIC-PASSIVE) and the projection operator $\Pi_{\mathcal{L}_1}$, no autonomous action $a_t^*$ violates hydrological lower bounds ($W_t \geq W_{\min}$) or FPIC on any parcel with $\mathsf{T}(i) = 1$. Formally: $C_{\text{Hydro}}(a_t^*, b_t) \leq 0$ and $C_{\text{FPIC}}(a_t^*) = 1$ for all $t$.

Remark 13.1 (Scope). G1 is conditional on A-FPIC-PASSIVE. Deployments in which this axiom does not hold must replace the fallback (Corollary 4.7) with a territory-specific protocol.

G2 (Economic efficiency). Under the concavity assumptions of Theorem 9.3, global capital $B_{\text{global}}$ flows toward low-cost, high-yield natural regeneration and ANR parcels, eliminating capital over-concentration in high-cost active planting.

G3 (Ecosystem autonomy and technological exit). Under Assumptions 10.1 and the slope/disturbance conditions of Theorems 10.3–10.5, once $E(t) > E_{\text{low}}$, intrinsic growth $f_0(E) > 0$ drives the parcel toward $E_{\text{mature}}$, and intervention intensity satisfies $\lim_{t \to \infty} \|I(a(t))\|_W = 0$. Under bounded disturbance, the ISS bound (Theorem 10.4) governs the ultimate deviation.

G4 (Basin-scale non-neutralization). Under the conditions of Theorem 11.1, restoration gains in $\Omega_{\text{rest}}$ are not neutralized by spillover into $\Omega_{\text{risk}}$, provided legal-territorial protection ($G_t = 1$) suppresses the leakage coupling $\bar{\sigma}$.

