15. Conclusion

We have integrated a distributionally robust, risk-sensitive, factored POMDP with (i) a measure-theoretic foundation grounded in Polish spaces and weak-* compactness; (ii) a governance-invariant FPIC algebra without belief-mass loopholes; (iii) a robust Bellman operator with a γ-contraction proof under sa-rectangularity and measurable selection; (iv) a recursive CVaR risk operator that composes with the robust Bellman operator by construction, plus a measure-based smoothing bound that shrinks with the smoothing scale; (v) a lexicographic multi-agent elimination protocol with finite-time convergence and scale-invariant disagreement; (vi) a Sovereign Data Supply Chain with an explicit oracle-trust boundary; (vii) a life-cycle economic layer with a corrected UAV/manual inversion threshold and equal-marginal MEROI allocation; (viii) a bistable Allee-type dynamics layer with Lyapunov and ISS theorems and a finite-time technological-exit theorem; (ix) a spatial drift–diffusion leakage model with a corrected net-positive-gain condition; and (x) a pre-registered six-phase experimental architecture.

The theoretical contribution is the joint formalization of risk-sensitivity, distributional robustness, minimum-sufficient intervention, dynamical self-sustainability, spatial non-neutralization, and Indigenous data sovereignty in a single decision algebra — with the mathematical edges of that algebra visible rather than hidden.

Brotherly closing note. The v6 draft corrects three substantive mathematical errors from v5 (the CVaR/Wasserstein domain mismatch, the Lemma 9.1 threshold conflation, and the Theorem 10.1 dimensional inconsistency) and three governance-relevant issues (the FPIC belief-mass loophole, the Algorithm 1 indexing bug, and the scale-dependence of the disagreement score). The remaining assumptions — A-FPIC-PASSIVE, A-BOUNDARY, A-BISTABLE, A-RECT, concavity of MEROI, and the empirical identification problems in §14.2 — are the places where the framework could break in deployment. They are flagged, not hidden. That is the correct posture for a sixth draft.

