Appendix A — Measure-Theoretic and Topological Foundations

A.1 The standing Polish-space assumption

Standing Assumption (SP). The following are assumed throughout:

· $(\mathcal{X}, d_{\mathcal{X}})$ is a compact Polish space; $\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{X})$ is its Borel σ-algebra.
· $(\mathcal{A}, d_{\mathcal{A}})$ is a compact metric space; $\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{A})$ its Borel σ-algebra.
· $(\mathcal{Y}, d_{\mathcal{Y}})$ is a Polish space (not necessarily compact).
· $\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X})$ denotes the space of Borel probability measures on $\mathcal{X}$, equipped with the weak-* topology induced by $C_b(\mathcal{X})$.

Proposition A.1 (Metrizability and compactness of $\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X})$). Under (SP), $\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X})$ is a compact metrizable space, and the weak-* topology on $\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X})$ is metrized by the bounded-Lipschitz metric
d_{\text{BL}}(P, Q) = \sup \left\{ \int f \, d(P-Q) : \|f\|_\infty \leq 1, \; \mathrm{Lip}(f) \leq 1 \right\}.

Proof. Prokhorov's theorem (Prokhorov, 1956): on a Polish space, tightness is equivalent to relative weak-* compactness. If $\mathcal{X}$ is compact, every Borel probability measure is tight, and $\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X})$ is compact. Metrizability follows from the fact that $C_b(\mathcal{X})$ is separable under (SP), so the weak-* topology is second-countable. The bounded-Lipschitz metric metrizes weak-* convergence on $\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X})$ (Dudley, 2002, Thm. 11.3.3). ∎

A.2 Wasserstein-1 metric

Definition A.2. For $P, Q \in \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X})$,
\mathcal{W}_1(P, Q) = \inf_{\pi \in \Pi(P,Q)} \int_{\mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{X}} d_{\mathcal{X}}(x, y) \, d\pi(x, y),


where $\Pi(P,Q)$ is the set of couplings of $P$ and $Q$.

Proposition A.3 (Kantorovich–Rubinstein duality). Under (SP),
\mathcal{W}_1(P, Q) = \sup \left\{ \int f \, d(P-Q) : \mathrm{Lip}(f) \leq 1 \right\}.

Proof. Villani (2009), Thm. 5.10. The supremum is attained; the class of 1-Lipschitz functions is closed under uniform convergence on the compact $\mathcal{X}$. ∎

Proposition A.4 (Continuity of $\mathcal{W}_1$). Under (SP), the map $(P, Q) \mapsto \mathcal{W}_1(P, Q)$ is jointly continuous on $\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X}) \times \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X})$ in the weak-* topology.

Proof. $\mathcal{W}_1$ metrizes weak-* convergence plus first-moment convergence; on compact $\mathcal{X}$, the first moment is uniformly bounded, so $\mathcal{W}_1$ metrizes weak-* convergence. The map is a metric, hence continuous in each argument, and by A.1 the space is compact, so joint continuity follows from separate continuity (Dudley, 2002, Prop. 2.2.3). ∎

A.3 Proof of Lemma 2.1 (Prokhorov-based weak-* compactness of the ambiguity ball)

Lemma A.5 (Restatement of Lemma 2.1). Let $\bar{T} : \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X})$ be a measurable transition kernel. For every $(x, a) \in \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{A}$ and every $\rho \geq 0$,
\mathcal{P}_\rho(\bar{T}(\cdot \mid x, a)) := \{ P \in \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X}) : \mathcal{W}_1(P, \bar{T}(\cdot \mid x, a)) \leq \rho \}


is weak-* compact.

Proof. Fix $(x, a)$. Let $\bar{P} = \bar{T}(\cdot \mid x, a)$. The set $\mathcal{P}_\rho(\bar{P})$ is closed because $\mathcal{W}_1$ is continuous (Prop. A.4) and $[0, \rho]$ is closed. Since $\mathcal{X}$ is compact, $\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X})$ is compact (Prop. A.1); a closed subset of a compact set is compact. ∎

A.4 Measurable selection: proof of Lemma 2.2 (Jankov–von Neumann)

Lemma A.6 (Jankov–von Neumann). Let $(\mathcal{S}, \Sigma)$ be a measurable space, $(\mathcal{T}, \tau)$ a Polish space, and $F : \mathcal{S} \rightrightarrows \mathcal{T}$ a set-valued map with nonempty compact values, upper semicontinuous in the sense that for every open $U \subseteq \mathcal{T}$ the set $\{s : F(s) \subseteq U\}$ is measurable. Then $F$ admits a universally measurable selector $\varphi : \mathcal{S} \to \mathcal{T}$ with $\varphi(s) \in F(s)$.

Proof. Standard; see Kechris (1995, Thm. 18.15) or Wagner (1977, Thm. 4.2). ∎

A.5 Proof of Lemma 2.3 (Measurability of the sup-map)

Lemma A.7 (Restatement of Lemma 2.3). Under (SP), suppose:

· $\bar{T}(\cdot \mid x, a)$ and $\rho(x, a)$ are continuous in $(x, a)$;
· $V : \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}$ is bounded and upper semicontinuous.

Then the map
(x, a) \mapsto \sup_{P \in \mathcal{P}_\rho(\bar{T}(\cdot \mid x,a))} \mathbb{E}_P[V]


is upper semicontinuous.

Proof. Let $(x_n, a_n) \to (x, a)$ in $\mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{A}$. By continuity of $\bar{T}$, the measures $\bar{P}_n := \bar{T}(\cdot \mid x_n, a_n)$ converge weak-* to $\bar{P} := \bar{T}(\cdot \mid x, a)$. By continuity of $\rho$, $\rho(x_n, a_n) \to \rho(x, a)$.

Consider the set-valued map $\Phi(x, a) := \mathcal{P}_{\rho(x,a)}(\bar{T}(\cdot \mid x, a))$. By Berge's theorem (Berge, 1963, Thm. VI.3.2), $\Phi$ is upper semicontinuous in the Hausdorff metric on compact subsets of $\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X})$ (which by Prop. A.1 is compact and metrizable).

For each $(x, a)$, the function $P \mapsto \mathbb{E}_P[V]$ is upper semicontinuous in $P$ in the weak-* topology because $V$ is upper semicontinuous and bounded (Portmanteau theorem). The composition of an upper semicontinuous function with an upper semicontinuous set-valued map is upper semicontinuous in the sense that
\limsup_{n} \sup_{P \in \Phi(x_n, a_n)} \mathbb{E}_P[V] \leq \sup_{P \in \Phi(x, a)} \mathbb{E}_P[V].


This gives the claim. ∎

