Appendix B — Dynamic Bayesian Network Factorization

B.1 Proof of Lemma 3.1

Lemma B.1 (Restatement of Lemma 3.1). Let $\mathcal{G} = (\mathcal{K}, \mathcal{E})$ be a DAG with parent map $\mathrm{Pa} : \mathcal{K} \to 2^{\mathcal{K}}$. Suppose the latent process $\{X_t\}_{t \geq 0}$ satisfies the two-time-slice Markov property: for all $t \geq 0$ and all $k \in \mathcal{K}$,
X_{t+1}^{(k)} \perp \!\!\! \perp \left( X_{t+1}^{(j)} \right)_{j : j \notin \mathrm{Pa}(k), \, j \neq k} \; \bigg| \; X_t, \; a_t.


Then
T(X_{t+1} \mid X_t, a_t) = \prod_{k \in \mathcal{K}} P(X_{t+1}^{(k)} \mid \mathrm{Pa}(X_{t+1}^{(k)}), a_t).

Proof. By the chain rule for probability densities,
T(X_{t+1} \mid X_t, a_t) = \prod_{k \in \mathcal{K}} P\!\left( X_{t+1}^{(k)} \mid (X_{t+1}^{(j)})_{j \prec k}, X_t, a_t \right),


where $\prec$ is any topological order of $\mathcal{K}$ compatible with $\mathcal{G}$. By the two-time-slice Markov property, the conditional distribution of $X_{t+1}^{(k)}$ depends only on its parents in $\mathcal{G}$ (at time $t+1$) and on $(X_t, a_t)$. Since all parents are at time $t$ (two-time-slice convention), this is $P(X_{t+1}^{(k)} \mid \mathrm{Pa}(X_{t+1}^{(k)}), a_t)$. ∎

B.2 Tropical forest parent map: consistency check

The parent map of §3.2 is reproduced here for reference:

Child Parents
$S$ $\{S\}$
$W$ $\{W, S, C\}$
$B$ $\{B, S, W, F\}$
$F$ $\{F, B, H\}$
$C$ $\{C, B, W\}$
$H$ $\{H, B, F\}$
$G$ $\{G\}$
$R$ $\{R, W, B, C\}$

Proposition B.2 (Acyclicity). The parent map defines a DAG on the two-time-slice graph with edges from $(X_t^{(j)}, a_t)$ to $X_{t+1}^{(k)}$ whenever $j \in \mathrm{Pa}(k)$ or $j = k$.

Proof. By construction, all edges are directed from time $t$ to time $t+1$. There are no same-time edges among the $X_t^{(k)}$. Hence the two-time-slice graph is acyclic (it is a bipartite DAG between layers). ∎

Remark B.3. The self-loop $\mathrm{Pa}(k) \ni k$ encodes the persistence of the $k$-th state coordinate. The map $B \to W$ and $W \to B$ are cross-edges in different time slices; they do not violate acyclicity.

