Appendix D — Distributionally Robust Bellman Operator

D.1 Well-posedness (Lemma 5.3, expanded)

Lemma D.1. Under (SP) and Lemma A.5, for every $(b, a) \in \mathcal{B} \times \mathcal{A}$ and every $V \in \mathcal{V}_b(\mathcal{X})$, the inner supremum
\sup_{P \in \mathcal{P}_\rho(\bar{T}(\cdot \mid b,a))} \mathbb{E}_P[V(b')]


is attained.

Proof. $\mathcal{P}_\rho(\bar{T}(\cdot \mid b,a))$ is weak-* compact (Lemma A.5). The map $P \mapsto \mathbb{E}_P[V(b')]$ is upper semicontinuous in $P$ (Portmanteau, since $V$ is bounded and $b'$ is measurable in $(b, a, Y, P)$ by Lemma 3.3). An upper semicontinuous function on a compact set attains its supremum (Berge, 1963, Thm. IV.1.1). ∎

D.2 Measurable selection (Lemma 5.4, expanded)

Lemma D.2. Under (SP), the map $(b, a) \mapsto \sup_{P \in \mathcal{P}_\rho} \mathbb{E}_P[V(b')]$ is upper semicontinuous, and the argmin over $a \in \mathcal{A}$ is universally measurable.

Proof. The first claim is Lemma A.7 applied at fixed $b$ with $(x, a)$ replaced by $(b, a)$. The second claim: consider the set-valued map $\Psi : \mathcal{B} \to 2^{\mathcal{A}}$ given by
\Psi(b) = \arg\min_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \left\{ c(b, a) + \gamma \sup_{P} \mathbb{E}_P[V(b')] \right\}.


The objective is upper semicontinuous in $(b, a)$; $\mathcal{A}$ is compact; so $\Psi(b) \neq \emptyset$ for every $b$. Upper semicontinuity of $\Psi$ follows from Berge's theorem applied to the upper-semicontinuous objective. By the Jankov–von Neumann selection theorem (Lemma A.6), $\Psi$ admits a universally measurable selector. ∎

D.3 Contraction (Theorem 5.5, expanded)

Theorem D.3 (Restatement of Theorem 5.5). Under (SP), (A-RECT), and Lemmas D.1 and D.2, the operator $\mathbf{T}^*$ satisfies
\|\mathbf{T}^* V_1 - \mathbf{T}^* V_2\|_\infty \leq \gamma \|V_1 - V_2\|_\infty


for all $V_1, V_2 \in \mathcal{V}_b(\mathcal{X})$.

Proof. Fix $b \in \mathcal{B}$. Let $a_1^*, a_2^* \in \mathcal{A}$ be minimizers of the $\mathbf{T}^* V_1$ and $\mathbf{T}^* V_2$ objectives at $b$ (existence by Lemma D.1 and compactness of $\mathcal{A}$). Then
(\mathbf{T}^* V_1)(b) - (\mathbf{T}^* V_2)(b)
\leq \left( c(b, a_2^*) + \gamma \sup_{P} \mathbb{E}_P[V_1(b')] \right) - \left( c(b, a_2^*) + \gamma \sup_{P} \mathbb{E}_P[V_2(b')] \right)


= \gamma \left( \sup_{P} \mathbb{E}_P[V_1(b')] - \sup_{P} \mathbb{E}_P[V_2(b')] \right)
\leq \gamma \sup_{P} \mathbb{E}_P[|V_1(b') - V_2(b')|]
\leq \gamma \|V_1 - V_2\|_\infty.


The second inequality uses the algebraic identity $|\sup_P f(P) - \sup_P g(P)| \leq \sup_P |f(P) - g(P)|$ with $f(P) = \mathbb{E}_P[V_1(b')]$, $g(P) = \mathbb{E}_P[V_2(b')]$. The third inequality is the sup-norm bound. By symmetry, the reverse inequality holds. Taking $\sup_b$ yields the claim. ∎

D.4 Value iteration convergence (Corollary 5.6, expanded)

Corollary D.4. Under Theorem D.3, $\mathbf{T}^*$ has a unique fixed point $V^* \in \mathcal{V}_b(\mathcal{X})$, and for any $V_0 \in \mathcal{V}_b(\mathcal{X})$,
\|V_n - V^*\|_\infty \leq \gamma^n \|V_0 - V^*\|_\infty, \qquad V_n = (\mathbf{T}^*)^n V_0.

Proof. Banach's fixed-point theorem on the complete metric space $(\mathcal{V}_b(\mathcal{X}), \|\cdot\|_\infty)$. ∎

