Appendix E — Recursive CVaR and the Risk-Sensitive Constraint

E.1 CVaR basics

Definition E.1 (CVaR). For a random variable $Z$ on a probability space $(\Xi, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P})$ and confidence level $\alpha \in (0,1)$,
\mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha(Z) = \inf_{\zeta \in \mathbb{R}} \left\{ \zeta + \frac{1}{1-\alpha} \mathbb{E}[(Z - \zeta)^+] \right\}.

Lemma E.2 (Rockafellar–Uryasev). The infimum is attained; the map $Z \mapsto \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha(Z)$ is convex, positively homogeneous, and 1-Lipschitz in $\|\cdot\|_\infty$.

Proof. Rockafellar & Uryasev (2000), Thms. 1–3; the Lipschitz property is standard (Shapiro, Dentcheva & Ruszczyński, 2009, §6.3). ∎

E.2 Recursive CVaR definition (Definition 6.1, expanded)

Definition E.3 (Recursive CVaR operator). For $V \in \mathcal{V}_b(\mathcal{X})$,
(\mathbf{R}_\alpha V)(b) = \min_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \left\{ c(b, a) + \gamma \; \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha^{P \sim \mathcal{P}_\rho} \! \left[ V(b') \right] \right\},


where
\mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha^{P \sim \mathcal{P}_\rho}\!\left[ V(b') \right] = \inf_{\zeta \in \mathbb{R}} \left\{ \zeta + \frac{1}{1-\alpha} \sup_{P \in \mathcal{P}_\rho} \mathbb{E}_P[(V(b') - \zeta)^+] \right\}.

E.3 Composition and contraction (Theorem 6.2, expanded)

Lemma E.4 (CVaR-robust commutation). For any $V \in \mathcal{V}_b(\mathcal{X})$ and any $\zeta \in \mathbb{R}$, the map
P \mapsto \mathbb{E}_P[(V(b') - \zeta)^+]


is weakly upper semicontinuous on $\mathcal{P}_\rho(\bar{T})$, and the supremum is attained.

Proof. The function $(V(\cdot) - \zeta)^+$ is bounded and continuous if $V$ is continuous, and bounded and upper semicontinuous otherwise. Portmanteau gives upper semicontinuity in $P$; compactness of $\mathcal{P}_\rho$ gives attainment. ∎

Theorem E.5 (Restatement of Theorem 6.2). Under (SP), (A-RECT), and Lemmas D.1–D.2, the operator $\mathbf{R}_\alpha$ is a $\gamma$-contraction in $\|\cdot\|_\infty$:
\|\mathbf{R}_\alpha V_1 - \mathbf{R}_\alpha V_2\|_\infty \leq \gamma \|V_1 - V_2\|_\infty.

Proof. Fix $b$. Let $a_1^*, a_2^*$ be minimizers for $\mathbf{R}_\alpha V_1$, $\mathbf{R}_\alpha V_2$ at $b$. By Lemma E.2, CVaR is 1-Lipschitz in its argument under $\|\cdot\|_\infty$. Then
(\mathbf{R}_\alpha V_1)(b) - (\mathbf{R}_\alpha V_2)(b)
\leq \gamma \left( \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha^{P}[V_1(b')] - \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha^{P}[V_2(b')] \right)
\leq \gamma \|V_1 - V_2\|_\infty.


Symmetrically for the reverse. Take $\sup_b$. ∎

E.4 Measure-based smoothing slack (Lemma 6.7 and Corollary 6.8, expanded)

Lemma E.6 (Restatement of Lemma 6.7). Under Assumption 6.6 (A-BOUNDARY), for every $P \in \mathcal{P}_\rho(\bar{T})$ and every $a \in \mathcal{A}$,
\mathbb{E}_P[|c_{\text{fail}}^\varepsilon(X, a) - c_{\text{fail}}(X, a)|] \leq C_{\text{bd}} \varepsilon_L.

Proof. By definition of $\varphi$ and $d_{\text{unsafe}}$,
|c_{\text{fail}}^\varepsilon(X, a) - c_{\text{fail}}(X, a)| = |\varphi(d_{\text{unsafe}}(X)/\varepsilon_L) - \mathbb{1}[d_{\text{unsafe}}(X) < 0]|.


The two values differ only when $|d_{\text{unsafe}}(X)| \leq \varepsilon_L$: if $d_{\text{unsafe}}(X) \leq -\varepsilon_L$, $\varphi(\cdot / \varepsilon_L) = 1$ and $\mathbb{1}[d_{\text{unsafe}} < 0] = 1$; if $d_{\text{unsafe}}(X) \geq \varepsilon_L$, $\varphi(\cdot / \varepsilon_L) = 0$ and $\mathbb{1}[d_{\text{unsafe}} < 0] = 0$. In the boundary layer, the difference is at most 1. Hence
\mathbb{E}_P[|c_{\text{fail}}^\varepsilon - c_{\text{fail}}|] \leq P(\{X : |d_{\text{unsafe}}(X)| \leq \varepsilon_L\}) \leq C_{\text{bd}} \varepsilon_L


by Assumption 6.6. ∎

Corollary E.7 (Restatement of Corollary 6.8). With $L_\varepsilon(\tau) = \sum_t \gamma^t c_{\text{fail}}^\varepsilon(X_t, a_t)$ and $L(\tau)$ its unsmoothed counterpart,
|\mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha^P(L) - \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha^P(L_\varepsilon)| \leq C_{\text{bd}} \varepsilon_L \cdot \frac{1 - \gamma^{T+1}}{1 - \gamma}.

Proof. By Lemma E.6,
\|L - L_\varepsilon\|_\infty \leq \sum_{t=0}^T \gamma^t \|c_{\text{fail}} - c_{\text{fail}}^\varepsilon\|_\infty \leq \frac{1 - \gamma^{T+1}}{1-\gamma} \cdot C_{\text{bd}} \varepsilon_L.


CVaR is 1-Lipschitz in $\|\cdot\|_\infty$ (Lemma E.2). ∎

Remark E.8 (Comparison to v5). The v5 bound contained no $\varepsilon_L$ factor. The v6 bound vanishes as $\varepsilon_L \to 0$ under Assumption 6.6, which is the correct trade-off: smaller smoothing scale, tighter guarantee. The trade-off is real only if Assumption 6.6 holds; if the failure boundary has positive mass (e.g., a discontinuity in the failure indicator not regularized by the signed distance), the bound does not shrink.

E.5 Static trajectory CVaR diagnostic (evaluation)

Definition E.9 (Trajectory ambiguity set). Let $\pi^*$ be the recursively-optimal policy from §6.3. Define
\mathfrak{P}(\pi^*) = \left\{ \mathbb{P}^{\pi^*, P}_{\text{traj}} \in \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{T}) : P \in \mathcal{P}_\rho(\bar{T}) \right\},


where $\mathcal{T} = (\mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{A})^{\mathbb{N}}$ is the trajectory space and $\mathbb{P}^{\pi^*, P}_{\text{traj}}$ is the law of the trajectory under $\pi^*$ and $P$.

Definition E.10 (Static trajectory CVaR). For a loss $L : \mathcal{T} \to \mathbb{R}$,
\mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha^{\mathfrak{P}}(L) = \inf_{\zeta \in \mathbb{R}} \left\{ \zeta + \frac{1}{1-\alpha} \sup_{\mathbb{P} \in \mathfrak{P}} \mathbb{E}_{\mathbb{P}}[(L - \zeta)^+] \right\}.

Proposition E.11 (Gap between recursive and static). There exist MDPs, $\alpha \in (0,1)$, and $L$ for which $\mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha^{\mathfrak{P}}(L) > \varepsilon_{\text{risk}}$ while the recursive constraint (A1) holds with slack.

Proof. Sketch: consider a two-stage MDP with binary rewards. The recursive CVaR constraint bounds the CVaR of the residual value; the static CVaR bounds the CVaR of the sum of losses. In general, $\mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha(X + Y) \neq \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha(X) + \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha(Y)$ — CVaR is subadditive, and the two can differ by the risk-diversification term. A concrete counterexample with Gaussian and Bernoulli components gives a strict inequality. ∎

Remark E.12. The diagnostic is reported at deployment but is not a hard constraint. The gap is an open quantitative question (§14.2, item 14).

