Appendix F — Multi-Agent Coordination Layer

F.1 Finiteness of the action space

Standing Assumption (F-1). The per-agent action sets $\mathcal{A}^k$ are finite. Then $\mathcal{A} = \bigotimes_k \mathcal{A}^k$ is finite with $|\mathcal{A}| = \prod_k |\mathcal{A}^k|$.

F.2 Scale-invariant disagreement

Definition F.1 (Range-normalized rewards). For each $k$ and $a$,
\tilde{R}^k(a) = \frac{R^k(a) - m_k}{M_k - m_k + \delta_\epsilon}, \qquad m_k = \min_{a' \in \mathcal{A}^{(1)}} R^k(a'), \; M_k = \max_{a' \in \mathcal{A}^{(1)}} R^k(a'),


with $\delta_\epsilon > 0$ a numerical floor.

Proposition F.2 (Scale invariance). If $R^k$ is replaced by $\lambda_k R^k + c_k$ with $\lambda_k > 0$, $c_k \in \mathbb{R}$, then $\tilde{R}^k$ is unchanged.

Proof. Direct computation:
\frac{\lambda_k R^k(a) + c_k - (\lambda_k m_k + c_k)}{\lambda_k M_k + c_k - (\lambda_k m_k + c_k) + \delta_\epsilon} = \frac{\lambda_k (R^k(a) - m_k)}{\lambda_k (M_k - m_k) + \delta_\epsilon} = \tilde{R}^k(a)


when $\delta_\epsilon = 0$. The floor $\delta_\epsilon$ breaks exact scale invariance but preserves rank order and bounds the denominator away from zero. ∎

Remark F.3. Exact scale invariance is recovered in the limit $\delta_\epsilon \to 0$; the floor is a numerical safeguard against degenerate cases where $M_k = m_k$.

F.3 Finite-time, non-oscillating elimination (Theorem 7.5, expanded)

Theorem F.4 (Restatement of Theorem 7.5). Let $|\mathcal{A}_0| = N_a < \infty$ and consider Algorithm 1 (v6). Then:

1. The sequence $\mathcal{S}^{(0)} \supseteq \mathcal{S}^{(1)} \supseteq \cdots$ is nested.
2. Algorithm 1 terminates in at most $|\mathcal{S}^{(0)}| \leq N_a$ iterations.
3. Every returned $a_t^*$ satisfies all constraints in $\mathcal{L}_1$.
4. If $L_2\_\text{satisfied} = \text{true}$, every returned $a_t^*$ also satisfies all constraints in $\mathcal{L}_2$.

Proof.

(1) Line 19 removes exactly one action from $\mathcal{S}^{(m-1)}$; no action is added. Hence $\mathcal{S}^{(m)} \subseteq \mathcal{S}^{(m-1)}$.

(2) $|\mathcal{S}^{(m)}| = |\mathcal{S}^{(0)}| - m$ until termination. Termination occurs at line 17 (if $m \geq M_{\max}$ or $\max_a D(a) < \varepsilon_{\text{conv}}$) or at line 21 (if $|\mathcal{S}^{(m)}| = 1$). In either case, $m \leq |\mathcal{S}^{(0)}|$.

(3) By lines 1 and 6/9, $\mathcal{S}^{(0)} \subseteq \mathcal{A}^{(1)}$, and every $a \in \mathcal{A}^{(1)}$ satisfies all constraints in $\mathcal{L}_1$. Eliminations only remove elements, so the constraint is preserved.

(4) If $L_2\_\text{satisfied} = \text{true}$, then line 9 sets $\mathcal{S}^{(0)} = \mathcal{A}^{(2)}$. By line 3, every $a \in \mathcal{A}^{(2)}$ satisfies all constraints in $\mathcal{L}_2$. Same shrinking argument. If $L_2\_\text{satisfied} = \text{false}$, then $\mathcal{S}^{(0)} = \mathcal{A}^{(1)}$, which may contain actions violating $\mathcal{L}_2$; the caller must invoke governance safe mode (§7.6). ∎

F.4 Complexity

Proposition F.5 (Per-iteration complexity). Under (F-1), each iteration of Algorithm 1 costs $O(|\mathcal{N}| \cdot |\mathcal{S}^{(m-1)}|)$ operations. The total cost is $O(|\mathcal{N}| \cdot N_a^2)$ in the worst case.

Proof. The inner loops at lines 13–15 each touch $|\mathcal{S}^{(m-1)}|$ actions, and there are $|\mathcal{N}|$ agents at line 13 and $|\mathcal{S}^{(m-1)}|$ disagreements at line 14. Total over $m$ iterations: $\sum_m O(|\mathcal{N}| \cdot |\mathcal{S}^{(m)}|) = O(|\mathcal{N}| \cdot N_a^2)$. ∎

