Appendix G — Sovereign Data Supply Chain

G.1 Differential privacy

Definition G.1 (ε-DP Laplace mechanism). For a query $f : \mathcal{D} \to \mathbb{R}^d$ with sensitivity $\Delta f$, the Laplace mechanism returns $f(\mathcal{D}) + \eta$, with $\eta \sim \mathrm{Laplace}(0, \Delta f / \varepsilon)^{\otimes d}$.

Lemma G.2 (DP composition). The composition of $k$ ε-DP mechanisms is $k\varepsilon$-DP.

Proof. Dwork & Roth (2014), Thm. 3.16. ∎

G.2 Zero-knowledge proofs: soundness and zero-knowledge

Definition G.3 (NIZK). A non-interactive zero-knowledge proof system for an NP relation $R$ consists of $(\mathrm{Setup}, \mathrm{Prove}, \mathrm{Verify})$ with:

· Completeness: for all $(x, w) \in R$, $\mathrm{Verify}(vk, x, \mathrm{Prove}(pk, x, w)) = 1$.
· Soundness: for all $x \notin L_R$, no PPT adversary can produce a proof $\pi$ with $\mathrm{Verify}(vk, x, \pi) = 1$.
· Zero-knowledge: there exists a PPT simulator $S$ such that for all $(x, w) \in R$, $S(x)$ is computationally indistinguishable from $\mathrm{Prove}(pk, x, w)$.

Remark G.4 (SNARK properties). zk-SNARKs add succinctness and non-interactive verification. Under the Fiat–Shamir transform in the random-oracle model, they provide simulation-extractability, which is required to prevent proof malleability in the ledger.

G.3 Oracle-trust boundary

Definition G.5 (Oracle-trust problem). Let $\hat{X}_t$ be the sensor-measured value, $\bar{X}_t$ the physical value, and $\hat{x}_t$ the committed value in the ZKP. The proof system establishes $\hat{X}_t^{(k)} \geq x_{\text{threshold}}$ (via $\hat{x}_t$), but does not establish $\hat{X}_t^{(k)} = \bar{X}_t^{(k)}$. The gap $\hat{X}_t^{(k)} - \bar{X}_t^{(k)}$ is the oracle-trust boundary.

Candidate mitigations (none currently specified):

1. Multi-sensor attestation. Require simultaneous agreement of $k$-of-$n$ independent sensors, each with a hardware root of trust (TPM or TEE).
2. Randomized spot-check ground truth. A fraction $p$ of reported values are re-verified by an independent field team.
3. Bonded challenger games. Any party may post a bond and challenge a reported value; if the challenge succeeds, the bond is returned with a penalty paid by the reporter.

Proposition G.6 (Bonded challenger incentive). If the challenger's bond is $B$, the challenge cost is $C$, the probability of successful challenge given a falsified report is $q$, and the penalty is $P$, then the reporter's expected payoff from falsifying is bounded by $(1-q) \cdot 0 - q P$, which is negative iff $qP > 0$. Hence bond-and-penalty schemes deter falsification provided $q > 0$ and $P > 0$.

Proof. Direct expected-value computation. The reporter's falsification gain is realized only when the challenge fails (probability $1-q$); otherwise the penalty is paid. ∎

Remark G.7. Proposition G.6 is a trivial incentive-compatibility observation; the practical challenge is ensuring $q > 0$, which requires sufficient auditing capacity.

G.4 Ledger integrity

Definition G.8 (Ledger block). A block $B_k$ is a tuple
B_k = \langle k, H(B_{k-1}), t_k, i_k, \mathrm{MerkleRoot}(b_{t_k}), a_{t_k}^*, \sigma_{\text{Gov}}, \sigma_{\text{Verif}}, H(Y_{t_k+1}) \rangle.

Proposition G.9 (Ledger tamper-evidence). If $H$ is collision-resistant and $\sigma_{\text{Gov}}, \sigma_{\text{Verif}}$ are unforgeable signatures, then any modification of a block $B_j$ with $j < k$ is detectable from $B_k$ with probability at least $1 - \mathrm{negl}(\lambda)$.

Proof. Standard blockchain argument. The hash chain $B_k \to B_{k-1} \to \cdots \to B_j$ cannot be re-derived without finding a collision on $H$; the signatures on $B_j$ cannot be forged under the unforgeability assumption. ∎

