Appendix H — Economic Architecture

H.1 Proof of Proposition 9.1 (UAV inversion, corrected)

Proposition H.1 (Restatement of Proposition 9.1). Define
D := \rho_{\text{density}} c_{\text{sapling}} + c_{\text{labor}} \eta_{\text{terrain}} + \frac{F_{\text{manual}} - F_{\text{UAV}}}{A}.


Then:

· If $D > 0$: $C_{\text{UAV}} < C_{\text{manual}}$ iff $\mu > 1 - (1 - \theta_{\text{target}})^{(q_{\text{rate}} c_{\text{seed}} + c_{\text{flight}})/D}$.
· If $D \leq 0$: $C_{\text{manual}} < C_{\text{UAV}}$ for all $\mu \in (0,1)$.

Proof. Compute
C_{\text{manual}} - C_{\text{UAV}} = A \left[ D - n^* (q_{\text{rate}} c_{\text{seed}} + c_{\text{flight}}) \right].

Case $D > 0$. $C_{\text{UAV}} < C_{\text{manual}}$ iff $n^* (q_{\text{rate}} c_{\text{seed}} + c_{\text{flight}}) < A D$, i.e.,
n^* < \frac{A D}{A (q_{\text{rate}} c_{\text{seed}} + c_{\text{flight}})} = \frac{D}{q_{\text{rate}} c_{\text{seed}} + c_{\text{flight}}}.


Using $n^* = \log(1-\theta_{\text{target}})/\log(1-\mu)$ (continuous relaxation) and noting both logs are negative,
\frac{\log(1-\theta_{\text{target}})}{\log(1-\mu)} < \frac{D}{q_{\text{rate}} c_{\text{seed}} + c_{\text{flight}}}.


Multiplying by $\log(1-\mu) < 0$ flips the inequality:
\log(1-\theta_{\text{target}}) > \frac{D \log(1-\mu)}{q_{\text{rate}} c_{\text{seed}} + c_{\text{flight}}}.


Rearranging:
\log(1-\mu) < \frac{(q_{\text{rate}} c_{\text{seed}} + c_{\text{flight}}) \log(1-\theta_{\text{target}})}{D}.


Exponentiating:
1 - \mu < (1-\theta_{\text{target}})^{(q_{\text{rate}} c_{\text{seed}} + c_{\text{flight}})/D},


which yields the claim.

Case $D \leq 0$. The bracket $A[D - n^*(\cdots)]$ is strictly negative because $n^* \geq 1 > 0$ and $(q_{\text{rate}} c_{\text{seed}} + c_{\text{flight}}) > 0$. Hence $C_{\text{manual}} < C_{\text{UAV}}$. ∎

Remark H.2. v5 stated the $D \leq 0$ case backwards (claiming UAV dominance). The correct statement is the above.

H.2 Proof of Theorem 9.3 (Equal-marginal allocation, expanded)

Theorem H.3 (Restatement of Theorem 9.3). Under the assumptions:

· (i) $\Delta E_i : \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} \to \mathbb{R}$ is concave and differentiable for each $i$;
· (ii) the risk term $R(u) := \gamma_{\text{risk}} \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha^{\mathcal{P}}(\sum_i L_i(u_i))$ is convex and differentiable in $u$;
· (iii) $w_i^{\text{prio}} > 0$ for all $i$;
· (iv) Slater's condition holds (the feasible set has nonempty interior);

then at the optimum $u^*$,
\mathrm{MEROI}_i(u_i^*) = \frac{\lambda^* + \gamma_{\text{risk}} \partial_{u_i} \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha^{\mathcal{P}}}{w_i^{\text{prio}}}, \qquad \forall i \text{ with } u_i^* > C_{\text{protection}}^{(i)}.

Proof. The Lagrangian is
\mathcal{L}(u, \lambda, \mu) = \sum_i w_i^{\text{prio}} \Delta E_i(u_i) - R(u) - \lambda \left(\sum_i u_i - B_{\text{global}}\right) + \sum_i \mu_i \left(u_i - C_{\text{protection}}^{(i)} \mathbb{1}(G_t^{(i)} = 1)\right).


Under (i)–(iv), Slater's condition holds and KKT applies. Stationarity in $u_i$:
w_i^{\text{prio}} \partial_{u_i} \Delta E_i(u_i^*) - \gamma_{\text{risk}} \partial_{u_i} \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha^{\mathcal{P}}(u^*) - \lambda^* + \mu_i^* = 0.


For $i$ with $u_i^* > C_{\text{protection}}^{(i)} \mathbb{1}(G_t^{(i)} = 1)$, complementary slackness gives $\mu_i^* = 0$, hence
w_i^{\text{prio}} \mathrm{MEROI}_i(u_i^*) = \lambda^* + \gamma_{\text{risk}} \partial_{u_i} \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha^{\mathcal{P}}(u^*),


which rearranges to the claim. ∎

Remark H.4 (Non-concave case). Without concavity of $\Delta E_i$, the KKT point may be a local rather than global optimum, and the equal-marginal condition is necessary but not sufficient. In that case, mixed-integer programming or global methods (e.g., branch-and-bound with convex relaxations) are required. This is listed as open problem §14.2, item 1.

H.3 Life-cycle cost functional: well-posedness

Proposition H.5 (Well-posedness of $J_{\text{cost}}$). Under (SP), bounded $C_{\text{diag}}, C_{\text{interv}}, C_{\text{maint}}, C_{\text{mon}}, C_{\text{risk}}$, and $r > 0$, the life-cycle cost functional
J_{\text{cost}}(\pi) = \mathbb{E}_{\pi, \mathcal{P}} \left[ C_{\text{diag}}(x_0) + \int_0^{T_{\text{hor}}} e^{-rt} (\cdots) \, dt \right]


is finite and continuous in $\pi$ under the weak-* topology on policies.

Proof. The integrand is bounded; the discount factor $e^{-rt}$ is integrable on $[0, T_{\text{hor}}]$; the integral is bounded by $(1 - e^{-r T_{\text{hor}}})/r$ times the sup bound. Continuity: dominated convergence under weak-* convergence of $\pi$. ∎

