Appendix J — Spatial Leakage Model

J.1 Well-posedness of the drift–diffusion PDE

Proposition J.1. Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^2$ be a bounded Lipschitz domain, $D \in L^\infty(\Omega; \mathbb{R}^{2 \times 2})$ uniformly elliptic, $v \in L^\infty(\Omega; \mathbb{R}^2)$, $S_{\text{ext}} \in L^2(\Omega \times [0,T])$, and $\mathcal{P}_{\text{enforce}} \in L^2(\Omega \times [0,T])$. Then the initial-boundary value problem
\frac{\partial \rho}{\partial t} = \nabla \cdot (D \nabla \rho) - v \cdot \nabla \rho + S_{\text{ext}} - \mathcal{P}_{\text{enforce}},


with $\rho(\cdot, 0) = \rho_0 \in L^2(\Omega)$ and zero-flux boundary conditions, admits a unique weak solution $\rho \in L^2(0,T; H^1(\Omega)) \cap C([0,T]; L^2(\Omega))$.

Proof. Standard parabolic PDE theory: Lions (1969), or Evans (2010, §7.1). Uniform ellipticity of $D$ gives the coercivity needed for the Lax–Milgram argument. ∎

J.2 Proof of Theorem 11.1 (Net positive gain)

Theorem J.2 (Restatement of Theorem 11.1). Under the assumptions:

· (i) $\int_{\Omega_{\text{risk}}} E(x,t) \, dA > 0$;
· (ii) $\lambda_{\text{defor}}(\cdot, t)$ is $E$-weighted-mean-integrable with weighted mean $\bar{\lambda}_{\text{defor}} = \int_{\Omega_{\text{risk}}} \lambda_{\text{defor}} E \, dA / \int_{\Omega_{\text{risk}}} E \, dA$;
· (iii) $\mathcal{L}_{\text{spillover}} \leq \bar{\sigma} \cdot \bar{\mathcal{P}}_{\text{enforce}} \cdot \int_{\Omega_{\text{risk}}} E \, dA$;

then $d\mathcal{G}_{\text{net}}/dt > 0$ if
\frac{\int_{\Omega_{\text{rest}}} (f_0(E) + g(E) u) \, dA}{\int_{\Omega_{\text{risk}}} E \, dA} > \bar{\lambda}_{\text{defor}} + \bar{\sigma} \cdot \bar{\mathcal{P}}_{\text{enforce}}.

Proof. By definition,
\frac{d\mathcal{G}_{\text{net}}}{dt} = \int_{\Omega_{\text{rest}}} (f_0(E) + g(E) u) \, dA - \int_{\Omega_{\text{risk}}} \lambda_{\text{defor}}(x,t) E(x,t) \, dA - \mathcal{L}_{\text{spillover}}(u, x).


By (ii), the middle term equals $\bar{\lambda}_{\text{defor}} \int_{\Omega_{\text{risk}}} E \, dA$. By (iii), the leakage term is bounded above by $\bar{\sigma} \bar{\mathcal{P}}_{\text{enforce}} \int_{\Omega_{\text{risk}}} E \, dA$. Hence
\frac{d\mathcal{G}_{\text{net}}}{dt} \geq \int_{\Omega_{\text{rest}}} (f_0(E) + g(E) u) \, dA - (\bar{\lambda}_{\text{defor}} + \bar{\sigma} \bar{\mathcal{P}}_{\text{enforce}}) \int_{\Omega_{\text{risk}}} E \, dA.


Dividing by $\int_{\Omega_{\text{risk}}} E \, dA > 0$ and requiring the right-hand side to be positive gives the claimed inequality. ∎

J.3 Corrected leakage model: discussion

The v5 model of $\mathcal{L}_{\text{spillover}}$ lacked an $E(x_j)$ factor in the integrand, which made assumption (iii) dimensionally inconsistent. The v6 model is
\mathcal{L}_{\text{spillover}}(u, x) = \int_{\Omega_{\text{rest}}} \int_{\Omega_{\text{risk}}} \sigma(x_i, x_j) \, \mathcal{P}_{\text{enforce}}(x_i, u_i) \, [1 - G_t(x_j)] \, E(x_j) \, dx_j \, dx_i.

Interpretation. The factor $E(x_j)$ is the vulnerability of the risk-zone parcel: fully degraded parcels ($E \approx 0$) do not contribute to leakage (there is nothing left to displace), and fully intact parcels ($E \approx 1$) are maximally vulnerable. The factor $[1 - G_t(x_j)]$ suppresses leakage where territorial protection is active.

Alternative formulation. If one prefers a model without the $E$-weighting, the net-gain theorem becomes
\frac{d\mathcal{G}_{\text{net}}}{dt} > 0 \iff \int_{\Omega_{\text{rest}}} (f_0(E) + g(E) u) \, dA > \int_{\Omega_{\text{risk}}} \lambda_{\text{defor}} E \, dA + \bar{\sigma} \bar{\mathcal{P}}_{\text{enforce}} |\Omega_{\text{risk}}|,


which is a different (and generally stronger) sufficient condition. Both are correct; the E-weighted version is the one stated in Theorem 11.1.

