Appendix K — Experimental Design Details

K.1 Power analysis for SPRT

Proposition K.1 (Wald's approximation). For a one-sided SPRT of $H_0 : \mu = 0$ vs. $H_a : \mu = \delta^*$ with type-I error $\alpha$ and type-II error $\beta$, the expected sample size under $H_a$ is approximately
\mathbb{E}_{H_a}[N] \approx \frac{(1-\beta) \log((1-\beta)/\alpha) + \beta \log(\beta/(1-\alpha))}{\mathrm{KL}(f_{\delta^*} \| f_0)}.

Proof. Wald (1945), §5. ∎

Remark K.2. For multiple hypotheses with alpha-spending, the boundary $\mathbb{A}$ is computed from the O'Brien–Fleming alpha-spending function
\alpha^*(t) = 2 - 2\Phi(z_{\alpha/2} / \sqrt{t}),


where $t \in [0,1]$ is the information fraction and $\Phi$ is the standard normal CDF. The boundary is then $\mathbb{A}(t) = \Phi^{-1}(1 - \alpha^*(t)/2)$.

K.2 Change-point detection for H4

Algorithm K.1 (Change-point estimation for $E^*$).

```
Input : paired (E_i, dE/dt_i) observations, significance level alpha_pre
Output: change-point estimate E*_hat and confidence interval

 1: Fit piecewise-linear regression of dE/dt on E with single change point at E*
    via maximum likelihood; also fit a no-change-point linear model.
 2: Test H0 (no change point) vs H1 (one change point) via likelihood ratio
    or Bayes factor.
 3: if H0 not rejected then
 4:     return "no change point detected; H4 not supported"
 5: else
 6:     Report posterior (or profile-likelihood) interval for E*.
 7:     Check dE/dt > 0 for E > E*_hat at significance alpha_pre.
 8:     Check d||I||/dt < -gamma_decay on the same interval.
 9:     if both checks pass then
10:         return "H4 supported"
11:     else
12:         return "H4 not supported"
```

Proposition K.3 (Consistency of MLE). Under standard regularity conditions (the change-point model is identified, the error distribution is absolutely continuous with respect to Lebesgue measure), the MLE $\hat{E}^*$ is consistent for the true change point $E^*$.

Proof. Standard change-point theory; see Csörgő & Horváth (1997), §2.2. ∎

K.3 Canopy attenuation identification

Proposition K.4 (Identification of $\mu_{\text{canopy}}$). Let $h^{(\text{drone})}(X_t) = \mu_{\text{canopy}} h^{(\text{clear})}(X_t) + \varepsilon^{(\text{drone})}_t$ and $h^{(\text{field})}(X_t) = h^{(\text{clear})}(X_t) + \varepsilon^{(\text{field})}_t$. Then $\mu_{\text{canopy}}$ is identified from paired drone-field observations if and only if $\mathbb{E}[h^{(\text{clear})}(X_t)^2] > 0$ and the errors are conditionally mean-zero given $X_t$.

Proof. Regress $h^{(\text{drone})}$ on $h^{(\text{field})}$: the slope is $\mu_{\text{canopy}}$ under the stated assumptions. If $h^{(\text{clear})}$ is constant (no signal), the slope is unidentified. ∎

Remark K.5. Under persistent cloud cover, drone observations may be missing not-at-random, violating the conditional mean-zero assumption. Identification then requires either instrumental variables or a selection model. This is open problem §14.2, item 3.

